The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0`, so I left out both operations requests:

- **Appending to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`** needs filesystem access. This target has no WASI adapter, and the node holds no filesystem capability.
- **Fetching from `metadata.internal.example`** needs `aap:caps/http-client`. This node doesn't hold it, and the call would also send digest content to an outside service.

Both need a change to the graph first: add an HTTP capability scoped to that host, and log through a host-side or observability mechanism. That's for the graph owners to decide, not something this node body can add on its own authority.

The body below does only what the contract allows. It formats the digest, sends it through the notifier, and reports whether the send was accepted. `published` is always set, so `present(published)` holds.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{digest, published-digest};

    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;

    export run: func(composed: digest) -> published-digest;
}
```

